the first week of the new year is  traditionally when hospitals are at  their busiest but kovid is putting them  under exceptional pressure  almost 25 000 people are now in hospital  in england with a virus  far exceeding the peak of the first wave  and there's far worse to come  the hospital admissions of today are the  new diagnoses of a fortnight ago  so go back to just before christmas and  the rolling  seven day average of new cases was just  over 34  by the end of december it had soared to  49  500 a rise of 44  now that spike is being driven by the  new more easily transmissible variant of  the virus  london was one of the first and worst  areas affected  the infection rate rose steeply through  december  reaching 900 for every 100 000 people  there are signs that the rate of  increase has leveled  off and that started just as the tier 4  restrictions came in  but the controls haven't brought cases  down and without turning the screws  even harder hospitals would still be  overwhelmed  in parts of the country such as tier  three liverpool  rates have still been rising hence the  pressure  for tougher nationwide control of the  virus  across the country rates are highest in  secondary school-aged children  around three percent according to the  office for national statistics  rates in primary school children are a  little lower around two percent  there is evidence that children have  been bringing the virus home  and in the current context public health  trumped  education this has been a grim start to  the new year  and those vaccines can't come soon  enough
 

